{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"COST CORRECTION, recorded mid-run — the plan's budget UNDERSTATES this run by roughly an order\nof magnitude, and the reason is a factual error in the plan's Budget section.\n\nTHE PLAN SAYS: \"30 cases x 2 arms x 3 epochs x 2 class slots = 360 case-runs, and every eligible\narm is single-prompt (the agentic multi-call arms are excluded above), so at most 360 model\ncalls.\"\n\nMEASURED: the case-run count is right; \"single-prompt\" is NOT. The exclusion the plan relies on\nremoves the three agentic DISPOSABLE-STORE reviewers, but the code-review arm is agentic too:\n`eval_solver._run_code_review_case` builds its RunRequest with\n`execution_mode=(prompt.execution_mode or \"agentic\")`, so each code-review case is a tool-using\nmulti-turn run. Eight of the fifteen sampled specs are code-review specs. The plan-review\ncriterion arm is likewise agentic wherever `registry.exec_tier(desc) == \"AGENT\"`.\n\nSo a \"case-run\" is not one model call. From the live logs at ~15% through\n(`REBAR_USAGE_LOG`, rows carrying both `model` and a non-null `input_tokens`):\n\n  standard slot, v1 arm:  16 case-runs -> 138 provider requests, 1,640,202 input tokens\n                          (~102k input tokens and ~8.6 requests PER case-run)\n  frontier slot, v1 arm:  27 case-runs ->  130 provider requests, 1,372,593 input tokens\n                          (~51k input tokens and ~4.8 requests PER case-run)\n\nExtrapolated to the full 360 case-runs that is on the order of ~25-30M input tokens and a few\nthousand provider requests across both slots, not 360 calls. The run was NOT stopped: it is the\nmeasurement this story exists to produce, the spend is the plan's own authorized shape (a live\nbillable paired corpus run), and aborting mid-way would forfeit the spend already incurred\nwithout producing the ParityReport. Recording it so the number is on the ticket rather than\ndiscovered later, and so S8 costs the cutover against a real per-case figure.\n\nSECOND MEASURED FACT, needed to make the run practical at all. Sequentially, one corpus item at\none epoch across BOTH arms of the standard slot took 224.0s wall clock (v1 82.5s / v2 40.9s).\nAt 30 items x 3 epochs x 2 slots that is ~6 hours. The harness therefore runs corpus items\nthrough a thread pool, with each worker executing under a context COPIED inside the\n`gate_config` + `gate_read_root` scopes. That detail is load-bearing, not an optimisation\ndetail: a bare thread inherits NEITHER ContextVar, and the mutation test proves the consequence\n— with the copy removed, every worker on BOTH arms resolves the ambient `claude-opus-4-8`\ninstead of its arm's model. That is exactly the split-brain this ticket's earlier comments warn\nmakes a parity verdict unattributable, and it is now pinned by\n`test_each_arms_config_reaches_every_worker_under_concurrency`.\n\nTHIRD FACT, from the same single-item timing and contrary to the N=1 pilot's hint that Bedrock\nwas ~35% slower: on that item Bedrock was FASTER — v2 40.9s vs v1 82.5s, with v2 making 7\nprovider requests to v1's 4. Latency is informational/non-gating either way; the full per-arm\nfigures are in the recorded results.\n"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785710636862570001,"uuid":"89dffb59-8dd3-4fdd-a860-d73d6aefb67b"}